DISC:
\paragraph{Strict models from the FPTM trainer}
At equal clause count, the strict models trained with the FPTM trainer are more accurate than the vanilla TM.
The only exception is NSL-KDD with 1,000 clauses per class, where the two test accuracies differ by less than 0.1 points (Table~\ref{tab:accuracy}).
They differ from the vanilla baseline in several trainer details and hyperparameters (Section~\ref{sec:method}), so we do not attribute the gap to one cause.
For \gainp{}, these strict models are attractive because each of their clauses costs one COMPARE \wosnew{cycle} in Mapping~S.

\paragraph{Mapping choice}
Mapping~S amortizes the program over all columns. It suits batch inference. Its latency does not depend on the batch size. \hyp{Its energy per sample is lowest when the batch fills the macro.}
Mapping~C writes the model once and leaves the input on the searchlines. It processes one sample at a time. For strict models, it needs only 3 array cycles per sample. However, strict models need large arrays and an external reduction of thousands of clause outputs.
FPTM shrinks both. On ALeRCE, the 20-clause FPTM needs about $47\times$ less array area and $50\times$ fewer reduction operations than the 1,000-clause $\LF=1$ model. The price is about 30,000 cycles per sample \wosnew{and, before the external reduction, \hyp{$1.6\times$ to $4.9\times$} more predicted energy per sample}.
For FPTM and strict models, Mapping~C is also predicted to cost more energy per sample than Mapping~S. Every sample activates the whole model array, and the controller energy is not shared between samples. \hyp{For FPTM models, the controller accounts for 14\% to 90\% of the Mapping~C energy at the conservative corner and 40\% to 97\% at the optimistic corner. Without the controller, Mapping~C still costs more than Mapping~S for every FPTM and strict model.}
